WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.000 --> 00:00:07.750
Ever run a program in the terminal that
gets stuck, and you hit Ctrl-C to kill it?

2
00:00:07.800 --> 00:00:10.640
That instant stop feels like magic.

3
00:00:10.720 --> 00:00:13.500
But it relies on a very
specific mechanism.

4
00:00:14.000 --> 00:00:18.100
When you press those keys, the terminal
sends a signal to the running process.

5
00:00:18.380 --> 00:00:22.780
This is an asynchronous notification
telling the program to act right now.

6
00:00:23.020 --> 00:00:25.040
The OS kernel is the primary sender.

7
00:00:25.300 --> 00:00:29.390
If your program accesses restricted memory,
the kernel fires a SIGSEGV signal to

8
00:00:29.440 --> 00:00:34.190
terminate it. It sends SIGPIPE for writing
to a pipe without a reader, or SIGCHLD

9
00:00:34.240 --> 00:00:35.800
when a child process ends.

10
00:00:36.040 --> 00:00:39.690
And one process can directly send a signal
to another process by using the kill

11
00:00:39.740 --> 00:00:44.690
system call. Signals are the standard way
the system forces a process to react to

12
00:00:44.740 --> 00:00:48.750
outside events. But getting a signal
doesn't mean your program has to immediately

13
00:00:48.800 --> 00:00:52.800
crash and burn. When a signal
arrives, your process has choices.

14
00:00:53.060 --> 00:00:57.430
It can accept the default action, ignore
the signal completely, or catch it using

15
00:00:57.480 --> 00:01:00.100
sigaction to run a
custom handler function.

16
00:01:00.560 --> 00:01:03.420
There is, however, a strict
limit to this freedom.

17
00:01:03.880 --> 00:01:07.850
SIGKILL and SIGSTOP represent the system
administrator's ultimate authority over the

18
00:01:07.900 --> 00:01:12.930
hardware. As the manual outlines, these
two specific signals can never be caught,

19
00:01:12.980 --> 00:01:16.720
ignored, or blocked by any process,
no matter how it is programmed.

20
00:01:17.040 --> 00:01:20.790
You can program a custom response for
almost any interruption, but the kernel

21
00:01:20.840 --> 00:01:24.160
retains the absolute power to
pause or terminate your code.

22
00:01:24.460 --> 00:01:27.640
Knowing when a signal actually
takes effect is crucial.

23
00:01:28.160 --> 00:01:31.460
Calling the kill system call doesn't
execute the interruption instantly.

24
00:01:31.540 --> 00:01:34.500
It only marks the signal as
pending inside the kernel.

25
00:01:34.840 --> 00:01:38.170
The kernel waits to deliver this pending
signal until the moment the process crosses

26
00:01:38.220 --> 00:01:41.460
back into user mode, like
after a context switch.

27
00:01:41.640 --> 00:01:46.190
And if your process is currently stopped,
newly arriving signals like SIGUSR1 are

28
00:01:46.240 --> 00:01:48.320
not lost. They are simply blocked.

29
00:01:48.680 --> 00:01:52.330
That blocked signal sits safely in a
queue, waiting patiently until the process

30
00:01:52.380 --> 00:01:55.220
receives a SIGCONT signal
and resumes execution.

31
00:01:55.580 --> 00:01:58.180
Signals do not just vanish into the void.

32
00:01:58.680 --> 00:02:02.130
Accounting for these delayed delivery
states helps you write predictable, stable

33
00:02:02.180 --> 00:02:06.730
software. Because a custom handler can
pause your program at any unpredictable

34
00:02:06.780 --> 00:02:10.910
millisecond, you must follow strict
coding rules to avoid corrupting your own

35
00:02:10.960 --> 00:02:16.450
memory. Inside a handler, you are only
allowed to use async-signal-safe functions,

36
00:02:16.500 --> 00:02:18.220
like the write system call.

37
00:02:18.320 --> 00:02:23.090
You cannot use printf. If a signal arrives
while the main program is already halfway

38
00:02:23.140 --> 00:02:27.410
through filling a printf buffer, your
handler will re-enter that exact same buffer

39
00:02:27.460 --> 00:02:29.620
and completely mangle your output.

40
00:02:29.980 --> 00:02:33.300
This precise timing creates
a classic race condition.

41
00:02:33.760 --> 00:02:37.270
Imagine your program checks a condition
variable, sees it must wait, and calls

42
00:02:37.320 --> 00:02:41.810
pause. If a signal hits in the microscopic
gap before sleeping, the handler runs

43
00:02:41.860 --> 00:02:46.540
early. The process then sleeps forever,
waiting for a signal it already received.

44
00:02:46.860 --> 00:02:48.920
The fix is strict ordering.

45
00:02:49.050 --> 00:02:53.730
You block the signal first with sigprocmask,
safely check your condition, and then

46
00:02:53.780 --> 00:02:58.350
call sigsuspend. sigsuspend works because
it replaces the mask and puts the process

47
00:02:58.400 --> 00:03:01.400
to sleep in one single atomic operation.

48
00:03:01.560 --> 00:03:04.500
The kernel guarantees no signal
can slip through the cracks.

49
00:03:04.860 --> 00:03:08.600
Let's review. Which two signals
can never be caught or ignored?

50
00:03:09.040 --> 00:03:11.860
The exact answers are SIGKILL and SIGSTOP.

51
00:03:12.080 --> 00:03:16.380
Why must you use sigsuspend instead of just
checking a variable and going to sleep?

52
00:03:16.500 --> 00:03:20.660
To prevent a race condition where the signal
arrives right before the sleep begins.

53
00:03:21.060 --> 00:03:26.240
Now open animation A6.1 in the
course hub and run lab TD5.
